window.docContents[24].resolve({"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Classical-Heat-Flow--The-Heat-Operator-and-Classical-Problems--The-spacetime-operator":{"contents":"With coordinate zero reserved for time, the classical heat operator on\n isHeatEquation.heatOperator is an ordinary function on Euclidean spacetime.\nIt computes the time-coordinate partial derivative and the Laplacian of the\nfixed-time spatial slice. Like Mathlib's Fréchet derivative, it is defined\nfor every u, and the analytic theorems state the regularity they need.\n\nSuppose  and  are differentiable at  and their fixed-time\nspatial slices are  at . Then the heat operator preserves their\nsum at . It also commutes with multiplication by a real constant:The scalar identity holds without regularity assumptions on , using the\ntotal derivative operators. These two calculus lemmas supply the\nsuperposition results for the homogeneous, forced, and initial-value problems.\n\nExpand . For a sum, apply the pointwise sum rules to\nthe time derivative and to the Laplacian of the fixed-time spatial slices,\nthen collect terms. For a scalar multiple, pull the constant through both\noperators and distribute it across the difference.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Classical Heat Flow\u0009The Heat Operator and Classical Problems","header":"6.2.1.1. The spacetime operator","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Classical-Heat-Flow--The-Heat-Operator-and-Classical-Problems--The-spacetime-operator"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Semilinear-Existence-and-Stability--Semilinear-Elliptic-Methods":{"contents":"Semilinear elliptic equations take the form . The\nlower-order nonlinearity changes the existence argument even though the\nprincipal part remains the Laplacian. For example,  comes from\na convex quartic potential, while  has a double-well potential\nand loses that convexity. The sign and growth of the source determine which\nof the methods below apply.\n\nWhen  is the derivative of a potential , the weak equation can be read\nas the first variation of\n\n\n\nConvexity of  makes the nonlinear part of the energy convex. The\nDirichlet term supplies coercivity, and the direct method produces a minimizer.\nDifferentiating at that minimizer gives the semilinear weak equation. This\napproach admits nonlinearities such as odd powers whose derivatives are not\nglobally Lipschitz, provided the necessary Sobolev integrability and\ndifferentiability statements are available.\n\nIf no useful potential exists, one may instead invert the linear elliptic\noperator and search for a fixed point, at the price of a smallness condition\nthat excludes many large or superlinear nonlinearities.\n\nMonotonicity lies between these viewpoints. A derivative of a convex potential\nis monotone, but monotonicity can also be used directly in stability and\nuniqueness estimates without referring to a minimizing construction. Ordered\niteration uses sub- and supersolutions to trap extremal solutions. The\nresolvents and Moreau envelopes treated here start from a differentiable\nconvex potential and produce Lipschitz gradient approximations.\nEvans (2010, Chapters 8–9) develops the variational\nand nonlinear methods;  (Chapters 5 and 9) emphasizes monotone\noperators and convex analysis.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Semilinear Existence and Stability","header":"9.4.1. Semilinear Elliptic Methods","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Semilinear-Existence-and-Stability--Semilinear-Elliptic-Methods"}});